00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.640
We're going to talk about magic squares of&nbsp;
squares. So first thing we're going to do is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.080
uh talk about what a magic square is. We're going&nbsp;
to start with a 3x3 grid; we're going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:19.680
to have 8, 1, 6, 3, 5 and a 7 and 4, 9, 2. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.380
this is a 3x3 array of numbers and&nbsp;
what's incredible here is that if you add the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.120
numbers down the columns - so like 8 plus 3
plus 4 - you're gonna get 15. You're gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.740
get 15 no matter what column you add down. Or if&nbsp;
you look at the rows and if you add those up then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:41.040
you also get 15, so all the rows add up to 15 and&nbsp;
also the two diagonals. So the principal diagonal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.260
- so this is 5 plus 2 is 7, plus 8
is 15 - and then if you look at the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:54.000
diagonal so 4 plus 6 is 10 plus 5 is 15. So&nbsp;
everything adds up to 15; so columns, rows and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.400
the two diagonals add up to 15. That's a perfect,&nbsp;
pure magic square. In fact this is the earliest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:05.580
you know up to some sort of rearrangement of the&nbsp;
of the numbers; it's uh it's the earliest known uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.800
recorded magic square. It's usually called the the&nbsp;
Lo Shu. There's records of it from about 2200 BC.&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.860
The Chinese tradition has it that the Emperor Yu&nbsp;
uh saw this magic square on the back of a turtle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:21.120
it's a sort of beautiful story.
